\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Real algebraic numbers are real-closed and metrically incomplete}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{Let #{A} be the set of real algebraic numbers, with the
metric inherited from the line. Every positive element of
#{A} has its positive square root in #{A}. Every polynomial
of odd degree with coefficients in #{A} has a root in #{A}.
There is a Cauchy sequence in #{A} with no limit in #{A}.}

\proof{
\p{Suppose #{\alpha>0} lies in #{A}, and let #{p} be a nonzero
polynomial with rational coefficients and #{p(\alpha)=0}. The
polynomial #{p(x^2)} has rational coefficients and vanishes at
the positive square root of #{\alpha}. That square root is
therefore real algebraic.}

\p{Let #{f} be a polynomial of odd degree with coefficients in
#{A}. For every sufficiently large positive #{T}, the values
#{f(T)} and #{f(-T)} have opposite signs. The intermediate
value theorem supplies a real root #{\rho}. Adjoin the
coefficients to #{\mathbb Q} one at a time. Each adjunction
is a finite extension, so the field #{K} they generate is
finite over #{\mathbb Q}. The coefficients of #{f} lie in
#{K} and #{f(\rho)=0}, so #{K(\rho)} is finite over #{K},
hence finite over #{\mathbb Q}, and #{\rho} lies in #{A}.}

\p{The nonzero polynomials with rational coefficients are
countable: each degree contributes countably many coefficient
tuples, and there are countably many degrees. Each has
finitely many roots. Every element of #{A} is such a root, so
#{A} is countable. Every rational is a root of a linear
polynomial, hence lies in #{A}. Enumerate #{A} as #{a_k}.
Begin with the open interval from #{0} to #{1}. At step #{k},
choose a nonempty open subinterval of length less than #{1/k}
whose closure lies in the previous interval and excludes
#{a_k}, and choose its center to be a rational number. The
centers form a Cauchy sequence in #{A}. A limit in #{A} would
equal some #{a_k}, which was excluded at step #{k}.}
}
